+# Privacy & Third-Party Audit
+
+Last reviewed: 2026-10-30. Re-run this audit (DevTools → Network, "3rd-party
+requests" filter, or a HAR export) whenever a script, embed, font, or image
+host is added.
+
+## Third-party inventory
+
+| Resource | Origin | Status |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Hero photo (Unsplash, photo-1495474472287) | images.unsplash.com | **Removed.** Now self-hosted at `/assets/img/hero.jpg`; loading it from Unsplash disclosed every visitor's IP, User-Agent, and referring page to a third party. |
+
+The homepage now makes **zero** third-party requests, and the CSP
+(`default-src 'none'` + `'self'` per resource type) enforces that. Adding a new
+origin requires an explicit CSP change, which is the review point for this audit.
+
+## Rules for adding anything new
+
+- Prefer self-hosting (fonts, images, libraries). If a CDN is unavoidable, pin a
+  versioned URL and use `integrity`, `crossorigin="anonymous"`, and
+  `referrerpolicy="no-referrer"`.
+- Embeds (maps, video) use a click-to-load facade and privacy-enhanced hosts
+  (e.g. `youtube-nocookie.com`). Social sharing uses plain links, never SDKs.
+- No analytics or marketing tags without a documented purpose. If they are
+  ever added they must be off by default and stay off when
+  `navigator.globalPrivacyControl` is true (we publish `/.well-known/gpc.json`).
+- Never put PII or session tokens in URLs or `localStorage`.
+
+## Data handling (server-side expectations)
+
+The static front end can't enforce these on its own; the backend must:
+
+- Session cookie: `Set-Cookie: __Host-session=…; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/`.
+- `/logout` accepts **POST** only (the nav uses a form, so it can't be triggered by
+  prefetching or a cross-site `<img>`), validates a CSRF token, and returns the
+  `Clear-Site-Data` header defined in `_headers`.
+- `/account/data` lets signed-in users download and delete their data without
+  contacting support.
+- Treat a `Sec-GPC: 1` request header as an opt-out of any sale or sharing.
+- Scrub emails, tokens, and IDs from logs.
+
+## Client-side behavior
+
+- `js/privacy.js` strips campaign click IDs (`utm_*`, `gclid`, `fbclid`, …) from
+  the address bar so they don't persist in history or shared links. It stores
+  and sends nothing.
+- No cookies, `localStorage`, or fingerprinting APIs are used by front-end code.
